```
=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
Router4 is not down and has not failed in any way. Direct inspection shows all
three active interfaces are UP (eth0 = 192.168.200.1/24 serving Location C,
eth4 = 192.168.15.2/24 to router5, eth5 = 172.25.1.1/24 to router3). FRR RIP
is peering correctly with both neighbours and has a complete routing table
covering all remote subnets. Every host in Location C (PC11–PC14) can ping
the gateway at 192.168.200.1 with zero loss, and PC11 successfully reaches
Location A end-hosts (e.g. PC4 at 172.16.10.4, three hops away through router5
and router0) with full connectivity.

The user's claim that "we can't get out to other sites at all" is also
incorrect for Location A. The only segment that is unreachable from Location C
is Location B (172.18.50.0/24 and 172.18.51.0/24, behind router6/mswitch2).
A traceroute from PC11 to PC15 (172.18.51.15) confirms packets transit
correctly through router4, router3, and router6 (hops 1–3), then are silently
dropped. The cause is an iptables FORWARD ACL on router6 that drops all TCP,
UDP, and ICMP traffic entering on eth2 (the WAN-facing r6_r3 link from
router3), with a single exception for HTTP from 172.16.10.0/24 to 172.18.51.1.
This is an explicit, standing security policy — the documented "B→A block" ACL
on router6 — and is not an injected fault.

Root cause: The ticket is based on a false premise; router4 is fully
operational, intra-site and inter-site (Location A) connectivity is intact, and
the only limitation perceived by the user (no reach to 172.18.5x) is an
intentional firewall policy on router6, not a network fault.

Corrective action: None required. Reassure the Location C users that their
router and inter-site routing are healthy. If access to Location B networks is
legitimately needed, that is a policy change request to be handled by the
security team, not a break/fix incident.
=== END ===
